\function defines a function, a block of lines that is
stored and executed every time the function is called.
Its syntax is the following:

\function name {
    \myosc.send /note $1 $2
}

The name is given without the backslash, and the function
is called with a backslash, like a command, optionally with
arguments:

\name 60 100

$1, $2, etc. in the body are replaced by the arguments.
A missing argument is replaced by 0. A function can also be
defined in one line:

\function name { \myosc.send /start 1 }

A function can give a value with \return, which ends the
function, so the lines after it are not run. Called inside
another command, the function is replaced by this value:

\function twice { \return $1 $1 }
\print \twice 7

The line above prints 7 7. \return without arguments ends
the function without a value, and a function without
\return has no value. Outside a function, \return gives
an error.

Defining a function with an existing name replaces its
body. Function names can be up to 50 characters long, and
can't be names of LiveLily commands.

Methods (after a dot):

\name.setargs 60 100

stores arguments, used instead of the ones the function is
called with, without executing the function.

\name.bind \inst1

calls the function at every note of \inst1, while the
sequencer plays. Instead of an instrument, it can be bound
to beat, barstart, loopstart, or framerate (or fr).
With an instrument, a number sets the step (note) of the bar
it is called at, every sets the steps between calls, and
times sets after how many calls it is unbound:

\name.bind \inst1 1 every 2 times 8

\name.unbind

stops calling the function.

\name.onrelease \other

calls the function \other when \name is unbound, after the
number of times set with times.
